STEP B -- client certificate without the CA in it                         done
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: pass the leaf certificate and the private key separately, so the CA is not
  part of the client credential.  Keep `ca_cert`, which is what checks the DUT's own
  server certificate.

$ tools/tb sudotool tb277 t9032r2 wpasup eth4 --method tls --identity cktls \
      --client-cert /tmp/t9032r2/cktls-leaf.pem --private-key /tmp/t9032r2/cktls-key.pem \
      --ca-cert /tmp/t9032r2/localca-rsa.cer \
      --expect success --timeout 45 --label R2-B-leaf-only \
      --pcap /tmp/t9032r2/T9032-R2B.pcap --json /tmp/t9032r2/T9032-R2B.json

OpenSSL: tls_use_private_key_file (PEM) --> loaded
SSL: Private key loaded successfully
                                                 <-- the key came from a PEM file, not
                                                     from the bundle

  Result: 17 frames, msglen=2141 again, to the byte, two pieces, same stop.

  Reading: taking the CA out of the client certificate file is not enough.  OpenSSL
  builds the client's certificate chain from its verify store when no chain is given,
  and `ca_cert` is exactly what puts the DUT's CA into that store.  So the CA goes back
  into the client's own chain.  STEP C removes it from the store as well.

STEP C -- leaf only, and nothing in the verify store                      done
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: same as STEP B with no `--ca-cert`.  The tool prints a banner on every such
  run saying the server certificate is then NOT checked, which is why this leg is a
  diagnostic and not the leg the verdict rests on.

$ tools/tb sudotool tb277 t9032r2 wpasup eth4 --method tls --identity cktls \
      --client-cert /tmp/t9032r2/cktls-leaf.pem --private-key /tmp/t9032r2/cktls-key.pem \
      --expect success --timeout 45 --label R2-C-leaf-only-nostore \
      --pcap /tmp/t9032r2/T9032-R2C.pcap --json /tmp/t9032r2/T9032-R2C.json

SSL: 1262 bytes left to be sent out (of total 1262 bytes)
EAP-TLS: Done
                                                 <-- the line "sending 1398 bytes, more
                                                     fragments will follow" is gone

  The wire, compared with STEP A and STEP B:

    client response id=7   STEP A and B: 1426 B frame, flags 0xc0 LM, msglen=2141
                           STEP C:       1286 B frame, flags 0x00
    DUT's next frame id=8  STEP A and B: 60 B, flags 0x00  -- an empty ACK
                           STEP C:       60 B, flags 0x80 L -- it carries TLS data

EAP: method process -> ignore=FALSE methodState=DONE decision=UNCOND_SUCC
EAP: Received EAP-Request id=8 method=13 vendor=0 vendorMethod=0
EAP: EAP entering state DISCARD

  Reading: 2141 minus 1262 is 879 bytes, which is the CA's 874 DER bytes plus 5 bytes of
  TLS framing.  The arithmetic closes, so the extra certificate in the earlier flights
  was the CA and nothing else.  The flight is now one piece, and the exchange still does
  not finish.  The DUT's next frame is no longer an ACK: it carries data.  That frame is
  the commitment message RFC 9190 requires under TLS 1.3, and wpa_supplicant 2.6 throws
  it away.  So the size of the flight was never the blocker.  TLS 1.3 was.

STEP D -- hold the supplicant at TLS 1.2 and authenticate                 PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: wpa_supplicant 2.6 has no setting for the TLS version, so the ceiling is set
  in OpenSSL, for this one child process only, through OPENSSL_CONF.  No root access,
  no package install, and /etc/ssl/openssl.cnf is untouched, so no other user of tb277
  is affected.

/tmp/t9032r2/openssl-tls12.cnf:
openssl_conf = ck_conf
[ck_conf]
ssl_conf = ck_ssl
[ck_ssl]
system_default = ck_ssl_default
[ck_ssl_default]
MinProtocol = TLSv1.2
MaxProtocol = TLSv1.2
