  => STEP D PASS: 18 EAPOL frames and EAP-Success at 1.047 s.  This answers the case
     objective directly.  The DUT started the exchange, offered EAP-TLS after the
     supplicant refused MD5, split its own 2339-byte flight into three pieces and waited
     for each ACK, rebuilt the supplicant's 2154-byte flight from two pieces, passed the
     exchange to the RADIUS server, and sent EAP-Success.  The supplicant checked the
     DUT's server certificate against a CA taken from this same unit.  The port then
     read Authorized with PAE state Authenticated, and the MAC entry moved from discard
     to forward.  Note that the successful flight needed two pieces, so splitting a
     client flight is handled correctly by both ends.

